    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity CAGR (0.4% 3y / 7.3% 5y) diverges >30% from revenue and EPS: book value is driven by buybacks ($40B+ since FY2023, $27B in Q1 FY27 alone, cutting equity to 38.4B) and the 2021 Slack stock deal, not by the business. EPS 3y CAGR is meaningless off the restructuring-depressed FY2023 base (0.21 -> 7.85). Revenue 3y CAGR (9.8%, with ~1pt of Informatica in FY26) is used as the anchor.",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF CAGRs exceed reported-FCF CAGRs because SBC fell from ~10.5% of revenue (FY2023) to ~9.3% (FY2026) while margins expanded after the FY2023 restructuring; the owner-FCF series starts from a depressed FY2023 base, so its CAGR is not a forward guide. Equity-award withholding taxes are only captured from FY2026 (351); earlier years' owner FCF is overstated by that amount. Owner FCF: FY2021 1,901; FY2023 3,034; FY2024 6,711; FY2025 9,251; FY2026 10,542."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Salesforce holds its ~20% CRM share and converts the agentic shift into consumption revenue: Agentforce + Data 360 ARR (~$3.9B, +210%) grows into a meaningful slice of revenue, roughly offsetting slower seat expansion in Sales and Service Cloud, so organic growth settles at 8-9% before fading. Informatica integrates on schedule and the FY27 34.3% non-GAAP margin inches up on internal AI productivity, partly eaten by token/inference costs (Anthropic spend). SBC declines slowly as a share of revenue; the leveraged buyback is financed, not repeated.",
    "bull": "Salesforce becomes the model-agnostic orchestration layer the Claudeforce partnership pitches: Data 360 + Informatica make it the trusted system of record agents run on, outcome/consumption pricing expands the TAM beyond seats, and growth re-accelerates to ~10-11% for several years. AI-driven internal productivity carries adj. EBITDA margin to 43% and lets SBC fall to ~6.5% of revenue. Requires Agentforce monetisation AND margin discipline AND no share loss to Microsoft - several things at once, hence the 25% weight.",
    "bear": "The 'SaaSpocalypse' partly lands: AI agents from Microsoft Copilot, OpenAI and Anthropic-powered startups compress seat counts and per-seat pricing, HubSpot keeps taking the mid-market, and Agentforce revenue cannibalises rather than adds. Organic growth drops to 3-5% after the Informatica lap, rising inference costs and price concessions pull adj. EBITDA margin to 35%, and SBC stays ~8.5-9% because talent retention in an AI market is expensive. A structurally slower business earns a 14x exit cap and 2% perpetual growth; the $39B debt load raises the equity's sensitivity to this path."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Salesforce is valued as a mature, highly cash-generative SaaS franchise on an owner-FCF basis: SBC plus equity-award withholding taxes (~9.3% of revenue) is charged as a real cost, the share count is held flat, and the Q1 FY27 debt-funded buyback is reflected once, through the new share count and the higher net debt. Acquired-intangible amortisation is glided down toward capex so its tax shield is not capitalised in perpetuity, and the Anthropic-inclusive strategic investment book is added to equity at a 25% haircut.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "AI agents disintermediating seat-based CRM licensing (bear-case axis)",
      "Leverage: $39.3B gross debt; a 1pp change in organic growth now moves equity value more than before the buyback",
      "Pending Contentful and Fin acquisitions: purchase consideration not yet in net debt",
      "Strategic-investment marks (Anthropic) are private and could reverse",
      "Inference/token cost inflation compressing gross margin if agent pricing does not scale with usage",
      "SBC staying near 9% of revenue rather than gliding down"
    ]
  },
